[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m# 4. What Worked Well[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 4.1 Architecture-first development[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe sprint demonstrated that repository governance can be designed before implementation begins.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe approved Execution Contract provided a stable implementation target and significantly reduced ambiguity during implementation.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 4.2 AI role separation[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThree independent AI roles successfully collaborated:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m* Architecture (planning, governance, approval)[m
[32m+[m[32m* Implementation (execution)[m
[32m+[m[32m* Independent Verification (compliance verification)[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThis separation reduced confirmation bias and improved implementation quality.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 4.3 Repository as the source of truth[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mImplementation relied on committed repository artifacts rather than conversational history.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThis significantly improved traceability and AI transferability.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 4.4 Independent verification[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mIndependent verification identified genuine contract deviations rather than implementation bugs.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mArchitecture reviewed those findings and distinguished between:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m* Blocking issues[m
[32m+[m[32m* Minor issues[m
[32m+[m[32m* Out-of-scope observations[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThis validated the governance workflow itself.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m# 5. What Did Not Work Well[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 5.1 Verification inputs were implicit[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe Execution Contract described implementation clearly, but did not explicitly define the verification baseline or expected verification targets.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mAs a result, additional instructions were required for the independent verifier.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 5.2 Verification authority was not explicitly defined[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mIndependent verification correctly identified implementation deviations.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mHowever, the verifier also attempted to determine Sprint completion.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mSprint completion should remain an architectural governance decision rather than a verification responsibility.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## 5.3 README onboarding required an additional iteration[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe initial implementation introduced the governance concepts but did not fully explain the contributor workflow.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThis required a verification-driven documentation refinement.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m# 6. Lessons Learned[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe sprint produced several important engineering lessons.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m1. Repository artifacts are more reliable than conversational context for AI collaboration.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m2. An approved Execution Contract provides a stronger implementation target than an implementation plan alone.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m3. Independent verification should verify contract compliance rather than redesign architecture.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m4. Architecture remains responsible for determining whether verification findings are blocking.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m5. Governance documentation should be designed for both implementation and verification.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
